##WORK IN PROGRESS##

Who was the client? What type of software did they want you to design?

The client was a company by the name of The Gaming Room.  They were in the market for a web-based game based on their hit Android game, Draw It or Lose It.  
The game was similar to the 1980's game "Win, Lose or Draw where teams would compete to guess what is being drawn.  T
he client wanted to be able to bring their game to multiple different platforms instead of it only being available as an Android application.

What did you do particularly well in developing this documentation?

I did well on describing the different parts of the UML Diagram.  My explanations would allow someone to have an understanding of how
the application works even without having access to the program or the UML Diagram.


What about the process of working through a design document did you find helpful when developing the code?
When I was developing the code while working through a design document, I found it easier to help keep track of the client's requirements
for the software which helped me ensure that I was creating software that the client was wanting.  Having the requirements neatly listed and kept up to date helped me remain focused on what I was working on
which helped me stay on track and pace for getting the project finished.


If you could choose one part of your work on these documents to revise, what would you pick? How would you improve it?

One section that I would revise or at least revise the way I approached it is the section about "Design Constraints".  When working on that section,
I only ended up thinking of a couple of things that could be considered a design constraint for the software and didn't really perform a lot of the out of the box
thinking about what different thinks could be a design constraint.  If I were to go back into it now, I would still consider the constraints that I already
chose but also try to use some of the information I have learned since then to try and think of some more design constraints.


How did you interpret the user’s needs and implement them into your software design? Why is it so important to consider the user’s needs when designing?


